  "_meta": {
    "what": "Who actually provides each civic service in each city — the fragmentation map. type = corporation (elected ULB) | state_board | spv (special-purpose vehicle, state-controlled) | state_dept | private | railways. The thesis: the high-value, capital-intensive services (water, transport, land) are pulled OUT of the elected corporation, which is left running the labour-intensive, unglamorous half (roads, waste, drains, lighting, parks, health).",
    "status": "Authored from public institutional fact, 2026-06. Entries marked verify:true are less certain (PPP/operator details, recent restructurings). 2026-06 revision: added the core in-house functions (roads, solid_waste, street_lighting, storm_water, parks, public_health) that EVERY municipal corporation runs — they were omitted in the first cut, which wrongly scored Chennai/Bengaluru at 0%. These six are 12th-Schedule functions the corporation retains even where water/transport/land are hived off. The department->complaint taxonomy (Ahmedabad CRMS style) is a SEPARATE, pending per-city portal scrape.",
    "services": ["water","sewerage","electricity","city_bus","brt","metro","roads","development_authority","solid_waste","street_lighting","storm_water","parks","public_health","police","fire"],
    "core_in_house_note": "roads, solid_waste, street_lighting, storm_water, parks, public_health are coded 'corporation' across the board: these are the bread-and-butter ULB functions retained even by the most hollowed corporations (arterial roads/NH excluded — those are PWD/NHAI; macro storm-water in some cities is shared with a state works/irrigation dept, noted inline)."
  },
